2025.10.23 - walkthrough session notes
Incoming channels for product application including those documents required for underwriting
• RM through an internal system
• Broker through email
• Paper-based forms and documents (in relatively lower proportion), scanned by an external
vendor in batch to provide the scanned underwriting documents
As-is process – 100% manual process for the underwriters to review all the underwriting documents,
prepare the underwriting summary and the assessment with the underwriting summary as the
rationale for the assessment.
Existing business pain points:
1. Takes up to a few hours for the underwriters to process each case with 30+ documents with some
of the documents with hundred of pages.
2. Potential risks of overlooking key information for the underwriting assessment because of the
volume of documents involved for each case
3. In some cases, can involve high number of turnarounds of supplementing documents
4. Can only support a very low % of manual audit of the accuracy of the underwriting assessment
To-be process:
• AI to automate document processing to generate the underwriting summary and assessment
with 100% human-in-the-loop for the final assessment considering the high protection coverage
typically involved. This is to address the pain points 1 and 2 above.
• The medium to longer term vision is to address pain point 4 above and then also an
underwriting workflow that can provide instant feedback to those who submit the underwriting
documents to reduce the no. of turnarounds of the documents.
Pilot scope:
• HK as the target market
• 2 insurance products for the HNW individuals, both are investment-linked products which have
both protection and also investment elements in the insurance products. Each with 15 – 20
document types associated with the product applications
• Only 6 – 10 document types will be covered in the pilot
AI pipeline:
1. Document classification
2. Document image quality check
3. Document criteria check / malicious document check
4. Document checklist verification
5. Document level summary per document (no fixed list of fields per document considering the
variety of the formats even for a particular document type)
6. ID and signature verification
7. Overall case level summary and assessment
8. Generation of the quality metrics (relevancy, consistency, fluency, coherence, etc) for the case
level summary
The current implementation has been focused on the step 5 – 7 above
Proposed workflow in the UI/UX
1. Document list showing list of documents classified, the image quality of those documents,
whether those documents meeting the criteria (e.g. whether bank statement is within XX days,
etc) or have any particular exceptions (e.g. any malicious documents with jailbreaking content
embedded, etc), whether there are any missing documents for the particular product applications
2. AI-generated document level summary with the corresponding preview of the original document
for verification of the accuracy of the summary
3. AI verification of the ID and signature across the documents where the user will provide feedback
on the accuracy of the AI verification
4. AI-generated case level underwriting summary, together with the quality metric of the summary
generated and the underwriting assessment (e.g. approved or not, any loading required, any cap
on protection sum, any exclusion of pre-existing conditions, any specific T&C per particular case,
etc)
5. User will assign an overall user satisfaction score at a case level based on the quality of the AI
generated content
6. Ideally, between versions, there can be a summary of what are the additional documents in the
new version and what are the changes in the content in the new version of the documents